T(M,"parallel-and-intersecting-lines","transversals-and-angle-pairs","Transversals and angle pairs","A road cut across two parallel railway tracks.\n\"The road is a transversal,\" said the teacher.",
 ["A line that cuts two or more lines is a transversal. When it cuts two parallel lines it makes pairs of equal angles: corresponding angles are equal and alternate interior angles are equal.","Co-interior angles (on the same side inside) add up to 180 degrees.","Vertically opposite angles at a crossing are equal, and angles on a straight line add up to 180 degrees."],
 ["transversal","corresponding","alternate"],("Find the other angle: co-interior angles, one is 70 degrees.",[("Co-interior angles add up to 180.","Rule."),("180 minus 70 is 110.","Subtract."),("The other is 110 degrees.","Answer.")]),
 ("corresponding angles are always equal.",["They are equal only for parallel lines.","The lines must be parallel."],["Forgetting the condition."]),
 ["Parallel lines make equal alternate and corresponding angles.","Co-interior add to 180."],
 ["m|Co-interior angles add up to|180|90|360|Rule.","t|Vertically opposite angles are equal.|T|Yes.","m|One co-interior angle is 70. The other is|110|70|20|180-70.","t|Alternate angles are equal for parallel lines.|T|Yes.","m|A line that cuts others is a|transversal|tangent|radius|Cuts.","s|Angles on a straight line add up to? Write only the number.|180|Straight.","m|Corresponding angles on parallel lines are|equal|supplementary only|90|Equal.","t|Corresponding angles are equal for any two lines.|F|Parallel only."])
T(M,"number-play","divisibility-rules-and-number-patterns","Divisibility rules and number patterns","Ravi wanted to know whether 3,456 is divisible by 3 without dividing.\n\"Add the digits,\" said the teacher.",
 ["A number is divisible by 2 if it ends in an even digit. It is divisible by 5 if it ends in 0 or 5, and by 10 if it ends in 0.","A number is divisible by 3 if the sum of its digits is divisible by 3, and by 9 if the sum of its digits is divisible by 9. 3,456 has digit sum 18, so it is divisible by 3 and 9.","These rules save time and help in finding factors."],
 ["divisible","digit sum","factor"],("Is 2,345 divisible by 5?",[("It ends in 5.","Check."),("The rule for 5 is last digit 0 or 5.","Rule."),("Yes it is divisible by 5.","Answer.")]),
 ("a number ending in 3 is divisible by 5.",["Divisible by 5 ends in 0 or 5.","3 does not qualify."],["Wrong rule."]),
 ["Rules for 2, 3, 5, 9, 10.","Digit sums test 3 and 9."],
 ["m|2,345 is divisible by|5|2|10|Ends in 5.","t|Digit sum 18 means divisible by 9.|T|Yes.","m|Divisible by 10 ends in|0|5|2|Zero.","t|A number ending in 3 is divisible by 5.|F|No.","m|Digit sum of 3,456|18|15|12|3+4+5+6.","s|Is 124 divisible by 2? Write yes or no.|yes|Even.","m|Divisible by 3 if digit sum is divisible by|3|5|2|Rule.","t|Divisibility rules save time.|T|Yes."])
T(M,"a-tale-of-three-intersecting-lines","types-of-triangles-and-the-exterior-angle","Types of triangles and the exterior angle","Meena folded a paper triangle and tore off its corners to put them together.\n\"They make a straight line,\" she said.",
 ["Triangles can be named by sides: equilateral (all equal), isosceles (two equal) and scalene (none equal). By angles they are acute, right or obtuse.","The three angles of a triangle add up to 180 degrees. An exterior angle is formed by extending one side, and it equals the sum of the two opposite interior angles.","So if two interior angles are 50 and 60 degrees, the third is 70 and the exterior angle at it is 110."],
 ["equilateral","isosceles","exterior angle"],("Two angles of a triangle are 50 and 60 degrees. Find the third.",[("The sum is 180.","Rule."),("50 plus 60 is 110.","Add."),("180 minus 110 is 70 degrees.","Answer.")]),
 ("the angles of a triangle add up to 360.",["They add up to 180.","360 is for a quadrilateral."],["Wrong sum."]),
 ["Angle sum is 180.","Exterior angle equals the sum of the two opposite interior angles."],
 ["m|Angle sum of a triangle|180|360|90|Rule.","t|An equilateral triangle has equal sides.|T|Yes.","m|Angles 50 and 60. The third is|70|110|60|180-110.","t|An exterior angle equals the opposite interior angles' sum.|T|Yes.","m|A triangle with two equal sides|isosceles|scalene|equilateral|Two.","s|Third angle if two are 90 and 30? Write only the number.|60|180-120.","m|A scalene triangle has|no equal sides|all equal sides|two equal sides|None.","t|A triangle's angles sum to 360.|F|180."])
T(M,"working-with-fractions","adding-and-subtracting-fractions","Adding and subtracting fractions","Anu ate 1/3 of a cake and Meena ate 1/4.\n\"How much did they eat?\" asked Mother.",
 ["To add or subtract fractions with different denominators, find a common denominator. The least common multiple of 3 and 4 is 12, so 1/3 is 4/12 and 1/4 is 3/12.","Then add or subtract the numerators: 4/12 plus 3/12 is 7/12.","Mixed numbers can be changed to improper fractions before adding. Always write the answer in lowest form."],
 ["common denominator","lowest form","improper fraction"],("Subtract 3/4 - 1/3.",[("Common denominator 12.","LCM."),("9/12 minus 4/12.","Convert."),("5/12.","Answer.")]),
 ("1/3 + 1/4 = 2/7.",["Make denominators equal first.","The answer is 7/12."],["Adding tops and bottoms."]),
 ["Find a common denominator.","Add or subtract numerators."],
 ["m|1/3 + 1/4 =|7/12|2/7|2/12|Common bottom.","t|We find a common denominator first.|T|Yes.","m|3/4 - 1/3 =|5/12|2/1|1/12|Convert.","t|1/3 + 1/4 = 2/7.|F|7/12.","m|LCM of 3 and 4|12|7|1|Multiple.","s|What is 1/2 + 1/4? Write as a fraction like 3/4.|3/4|2/4+1/4.","m|Answers should be in|lowest form|any form|decimals only|Simplify.","t|Mixed numbers can become improper fractions.|T|Yes."])
